<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Copyright 2026 stevej52
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. See LICENSE.

  Layer 3: ros2_control, so the simulated robot can be driven.

  Eight joints. The four steering knuckles take POSITION commands, because a
  servo is commanded to an angle; the four wheels take VELOCITY commands,
  because an ESC is commanded to a speed. That mirrors the real machine, where
  ros2_pca9685 drives three PWM channels: a throttle, a front steering servo
  and a rear steering servo.

  WHERE THIS DIFFERS FROM THE REAL DRIVETRAIN, and it matters on a crawler.

  The robot is four-wheel DRIVE from a SINGLE motor: one ESC on channel 0, a
  centre shaft, and a differential on each axle. The wheels end up at
  different speeds through a turn because the differentials let them, not
  because anything commands them to.

  Here each wheel is commanded to its own velocity independently. The speeds
  that come out match what the differentials would produce, so a turn looks
  right - but the failure mode does not. With an open differential, a wheel
  that lifts or loses grip takes all the torque and the robot stops dead; that
  is THE characteristic way a rock crawler gets stuck. A velocity-commanded
  joint is forced to its speed whatever the traction, so the simulated robot
  cannot wheelspin and cannot lose drive to a lifted wheel.

  The consequence, stated plainly so nobody is surprised by it: THIS ROBOT
  CLIMBS BETTER IN SIMULATION THAN IT DOES ON ROCKS. Use the simulator for
  navigation, SLAM, odometry and tilt behaviour, where it is faithful. Do not
  use it to answer "can the robot get up this", and do not tune traction
  against it.

  Tilt thresholds are unaffected: driving onto a 30 degree ramp tilts the
  robot 30 degrees whether or not the wheels are slipping, so tuning
  tilt_guard here is still valid.

  Modelling it properly would mean effort commands and one torque source split
  across the axles. That is a real piece of work and is not layer 3.

  Deliberately NOT ackermann_steering_controller. That controller models two
  steered wheels and two driven ones - a front-steering car. This chassis
  steers all four, which it cannot express. Instead the joints are exposed
  through plain forward-command controllers and the kinematics live in
  steering.py, where they are unit-tested against closed-form geometry rather
  than eyeballed in a simulator.

  Plugin and controller class names were read out of the installed packages
  before being written here, not recalled:
    gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem              gz_hardware_plugins.xml
    position_controllers/JointGroupPositionController
    velocity_controllers/JointGroupVelocityController
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <xacro:macro name="steer_joint_control" params="name">
    <joint name="${name}_steer_joint">
      <command_interface name="position">
        <!-- Matches the URDF's own limit: 30 degrees of servo travel. -->
        <param name="min">-0.5236</param>
        <param name="max">0.5236</param>
      </command_interface>
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <xacro:macro name="wheel_joint_control" params="name">
    <joint name="${name}_wheel_joint">
      <command_interface name="velocity"/>
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
    </joint>
  </xacro:macro>

  <ros2_control name="GazeboSimSystem" type="system">
    <hardware>
      <plugin>gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem</plugin>
    </hardware>

    <xacro:steer_joint_control name="front_left"/>
    <xacro:steer_joint_control name="front_right"/>
    <xacro:steer_joint_control name="rear_left"/>
    <xacro:steer_joint_control name="rear_right"/>

    <xacro:wheel_joint_control name="front_left"/>
    <xacro:wheel_joint_control name="front_right"/>
    <xacro:wheel_joint_control name="rear_left"/>
    <xacro:wheel_joint_control name="rear_right"/>
  </ros2_control>

  <gazebo>
    <plugin filename="gz_ros2_control-system"
            name="gz_ros2_control::GazeboSimROS2ControlPlugin">
      <parameters>$(find jetnano_gazebo)/config/controllers.yaml</parameters>
      <!--
        The controller_manager runs inside Gazebo and must use Gazebo's clock,
        or every controller timestamps its commands from a different clock
        than the physics they act on.
      -->
      <ros>
        <namespace>/</namespace>
      </ros>
    </plugin>
  </gazebo>

</robot>
